Correct! It portrays a ruler or high-status figure.
[[Next->Africa Q2]]



:: Africa Q1 study {"position":"1400,560","size":"100,100"}
(set: $tries to 0)
*History:* The Ife heads are thought to portray rulers (the Ooni) or other high-status individuals from the ancient city of Ife, the spiritual heartland of the Yoruba people. The elaborate crowns and formal naturalism identify them as portraits of power, not ordinary citizens.

[[Continue->Africa Q2]]



:: Africa Q2 {"position":"1400,690","size":"100,100"}
*Burton poses a question.*
**Question 2 of 4: Why is this Ife head historically significant?**

[[It shows the high artistic skill of Ife, Nigeria, and challenges the old European idea that complex naturalistic sculpture was mainly a feature of ancient Greece or Rome->Africa Q2 ok]]

(link: "It proves that Ife artists copied Egyptian royal portraits during the reign of Ramesses II")[ (goto: "Africa Q2 no") ]

(link: "It was supposedly the first African object ever collected by the British Museum")[ (goto: "Africa Q2 no") ]

(link: "It was supposedly used as a modern theatrical mask in Britain")[ (goto: "Africa Q2 no") ]



:: Africa Q2 no {"position":"1400,950","size":"100,100"}
(set: $tries to $tries + 1)
(if: $tries >= 2)[
*Burton:* "Not to worry — being wrong is part of the journey. Study the answer, or move on."
(link: "STUDY THE INFORMATION")[ (set: $tries to 0) (goto: "Africa Q2 study") ]
(link: "MOVE ON")[ (set: $tries to 0) (goto: "Africa Q3") ]
](else:)[
*Burton:* "When European scholars first saw these heads, they refused to believe Africans had made them — what does that tell you?"
[[Try again->Africa Q2]]
]



:: Africa Q2 ok {"position":"1400,820","size":"100,100"}
(set: $tries to 0)
(set: $score to it + 10)
Correct! It challenges Eurocentric assumptions about naturalistic art.
[[Next->Africa Q3]]



:: Africa Q2 study {"position":"1400,1080","size":"100,100"}
(set: $tries to 0)
*History:* When Ife heads became known to the wider world in the early 20th century, their naturalism was so remarkable that some Europeans falsely attributed them to outside influences. In fact they are the product of a sophisticated West African artistic tradition, and they powerfully challenge Eurocentric assumptions about where complex naturalistic sculpture could be created.

[[Continue->Africa Q3]]



:: Africa Q3 {"position":"1400,1210","size":"100,100"}
*Burton poses a question.*
**Question 3 of 4: Burton turns to a carved ceremonial mask. What was one possible function of this mask?**

[[It may have been used to discourage unruly behaviour and was associated with a society that exercised judicial powers->Africa Q3 ok]]

(link: "It may have been a public symbol during harvest festivals celebrating agricultural abundance")[ (goto: "Africa Q3 no") ]

(link: "It may have been used in initiation ceremonies to teach young members about social rules")[ (goto: "Africa Q3 no") ]

(link: "It may have been worn by warriors during victory ceremonies to display courage")[ (goto: "Africa Q3 no") ]



