:: Egypt Q1 study {"position":"1100,560","size":"100,100"}
(set: $tries to 0)
*History:* The Younger Memnon depicts Ramesses II ('the Great'), who ruled ancient Egypt for 66 years. It was carved from a single piece of two-coloured granite and once stood at his memorial temple, the Ramesseum, on the west bank of the Nile at Thebes.

[[Continue->Egypt Q2]]



:: Egypt Q2 {"position":"1100,690","size":"100,100"}
*Burton poses a question.*
**Question 2 of 4: Looking at the head of "The Younger Memnon", which feature identifies the figure as a pharaoh, and what does it suggest about how Ramesses II wanted to be represented?**

[[He wears the nemes headcloth, a royal headdress associated with Egyptian kingship, suggesting the statue presents him not as an ordinary man but as a powerful and legitimate ruler->Egypt Q2 ok]]

(link: "He wears the khepresh crown, often connected with warfare, suggesting the statue mainly presents him as a military commander")[ (goto: "Egypt Q2 no") ]

(link: "He wears the hedjet crown, the white crown of Upper Egypt, suggesting the statue emphasizes his control over southern Egypt only")[ (goto: "Egypt Q2 no") ]

(link: "He wears the deshret crown, the red crown of Lower Egypt, suggesting he is mainly a regional ruler of the Nile Delta")[ (goto: "Egypt Q2 no") ]



