*History:* The nemes headcloth — the striped linen cloth pulled tight across the forehead and falling over the shoulders — is one of the most recognisable emblems of Egyptian kingship. Its presence signals that the statue presents Ramesses not merely as a man but as the embodiment of legitimate royal power.

[[Continue->Egypt Q3]]



:: Egypt Q3 {"position":"1100,1210","size":"100,100"}
*Burton poses a question.*
**Question 3 of 4: Why is the Rosetta Stone considered such a crucial object for the development of Egyptology, rather than simply an important ancient inscription?**

[[Because it preserves the same decree in hieroglyphic, Demotic, and Greek scripts, letting scholars compare them and use the Greek version to help decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs->Egypt Q3 ok]]

(link: "Because it is the oldest surviving Egyptian monument and the first known example of Egyptian writing")[ (goto: "Egypt Q3 no") ]

(link: "Because it contains a complete biography of Tutankhamun, allowing historians to reconstruct the Eighteenth Dynasty")[ (goto: "Egypt Q3 no") ]

(link: "Because it was discovered inside a royal pyramid, proving pyramids were used mainly as archives for political documents")[ (goto: "Egypt Q3 no") ]



